 in order to draw conclusions  and give answers  everything we do in life is governed by  past experience  events in our past and specific things  we have learned  act as mental guides so we can solve  mental problems  therefore whenever we face a new task  and especially if this task  requires new forms of knowledge or  thinking  our mind fails to recognize this process  as familiar  and decides to follow the path of least  resistance  so whenever you feel resistance creeping  in don't get frustrated  it is a very natural process and it  happens to everyone  the thing is that with the right mindset  it can't be overcome  procrastination is a natural human  tendency no matter what you do  you can't always be in work mode there  are times where you just need to chill  do nothing or inject your brain with  small doses of dopamine  and that's okay your brain is not a  machine from the almost 16 hours you're  awake usually four to five hours and  maybe even less you're able to work in  deep work mode  what we're trying to accomplish here is  to efficiently use these hours  in order to finish your most challenging  tasks  and when it comes to overcoming  procrastination procrastination is not  really your problem  your mindset is your problem  procrastination should be perceived as a  reward  a reward for your hard work when you  finish your important tasks  you can allow yourself to procrastinate  non-judgmentally  and that is a very liberating feeling  the way you maximize efficacy isn't  rocket science  it is actually quite simple and  straightforward efficacy is all about  doing the right things  so whatever life scenario you find  yourself into there is definitely  one project or task in your life that  stands out and you consider  more important than others what you need  to do is give  all your attention to this project for  two to three hours per day  that's it whether your project is to  prepare for a big exam  a big presentation a pitch an  application  dedicate two to three hours per day and  you're good to go  as tim ferry said in one of his blog  posts block out  at least two to three hours to focus on  one activity for today  let the rest of the urgent but less  important stuff slide  it will still be there tomorrow  if you're going to work on your computer  you should focus on the programs and  websites  that are relevant to your work the rest  of the programs and websites  should remain closed no instagram no  twitter no news sites no email  some people suggest using apps that help  you stay disconnected from the internet  or allow you to access websites  only relevant to your work but i don't  really use them because i see that as an  exercise in self-discipline  also try to avoid meetings during that  time and if something comes up  inform the party who distracted you that  you need to contact them later  the most difficult part of every task is  the first minutes  it is the time when you face your  creative block and resistance is raising  a huge wall between you  and your ability to enter flow the thing  is that you can't expect your brain to  enter flow immediately  you need to provide it with some small  bytes of information  relevant to your task in order to  process that information  and eventually allow you to get immersed  in the activity  for example when i start writing i feel  like a crippled person with a crayon in  my mouth  a skirt to a bone good used to say the  words won't come out and i will struggle  a lot  after 10 to 15 minutes however the words  will come out  naturally the sentences will be formed  effortlessly  and the writing process will feel  extremely enjoyable  so whatever your task is start small  allow yourself to produce a not so  special result in the beginning  and once you enter flow you will be  amazed by your results  as i said it usually takes 10 to 15  minutes to enter flow  once you're there everything you do  happens naturally non-judgmentally  and with a great sense of self-worth  this happens because in flow states  we switch off our dorsolateral frontal  cortex  this is the part of our brain that  allows us to analyze and question our  own behavior  which can be highly useful but  unfortunately  it also slows everything down because it  means that all our decisions have to go  through it and it can act as a creative  barrier  by switching off this part we allow all  our attention to go to the part we want  to use  without censorship or micromanagement  this results in quick reactions and  uninterrupted  productivity flow state has fluctuations  but  once you're there you're actually aware  of it and you can become  consciously productive however because  it is a quite an energy draining state  we can't stay there long so reaching it  even for small time frames during these  two to three hours  can help us achieve great results  as we progress towards the future we  need to be careful with the words we use  to  shape our reality we all want to do  things that we find  interesting and enjoyable i don't think  that anybody  procrastinated while doing things they  find interesting and enjoyable  the problem is that we spend a lot of  time on things we don't like  just because we have to let's at least  not feel guilty when we avoid those  things  let's at least be more caring to  ourselves when we  don't feel like doing those things  hey guys so this video is sponsored by  skillshare a skillshare is an online  learning community with thousands of  inspiring classes for creators  where you can explore new skills deepen  existing passions  and get lost in creativity as you have  probably realized after watching the  video  i have brooded a lot over the idea of  procrastination i am convinced that  we cannot really avoid procrastination  so  let's enjoy it by making sure that we  are productive during the hours we want  to be productive  i think skillshare is an exceptional  tool for this especially when it comes  to battling resistance an